README-SHELLS -- five shells, and which one to takeTake `bash' to type at, and `ksh' for $SHELL.  `sh' is the smallest realBourne shell.  `gshell' and `mshell' are full-screen menus.THE FIVE--------  file      bytes   needs   module   source            docs                     cio     name  ------------------------------------------------------------------------  bash     242604    no     bash     -                 DOC/bash (3 files)  sh        77306    no     sh       -                 DOC/sh (4 files)  ksh      139824    no     ksh      SRC/pdksh (176)   DOC/ksh, DOC/pdksh,                                                       DOC/README-KSH  gshell    23114    no     gshell   SRC/eff_gshell    DOC/gshell (4 files)  mshell     7228    yes    mshell   -                 DOC/mshell  bash    GNU Bourne-Again Shell 1.12, the one SYS/login starts  sh      Bourne shell v7.5, from the sh75 package  ksh     pdksh, the public-domain Korn shell  gshell  GSHELL V1.1, 1988 -- a menu  mshell  a menu shell driven by a menu fileCHOOSING--------  You want a shell to work in                            bash  You want $SHELL to name a shell that can parse         ksh  You want the smallest real Bourne shell                sh  You want a real `chd' that moves the data directory    sh  You have no cio and cannot get one                     bash, sh or ksh  You want a menu for somebody else to use               gshell  One file: `bash'.  Two: `bash' and `ksh', with SHELL set to the ksh --  SYS/login on this disk shows the line.bash -- the one to type at--------------------------  Command history, job control, functions, and scripts that come across  from Unix unchanged.  /dd/.bashrc is read because SYS/login points HOME  at this disk.  Two of its built-ins are replaced in .bashrc and must stay replaced.  bash's own `pwd' hangs the shell, and where /dd is not an RBF device  its `cd' aborts with a bus error: getwd() walks `..' looking for a  single root, and OS-9 has one root per device.  The replacements track  the path as a string.  So bash's `cd' does not move the OS-9 data directory.  After  `cd somewhere; prog', a program that opens a file by bare name looks  where the data directory already was.  Costs: twice the size of the next largest, and it cannot be $SHELL.sh -- the small real Bourne shell---------------------------------  A third of bash's size, no cio, and a real `chd': `pwd' reports a  directory that actually moved.  It cannot fork a program by absolute pathname under os9exec:  `sh -c '/dd/CMDS/wc -c file'` answers `file not found' for a file that  exists, with PATH set and `chx' done.  So it cannot launch most of this  collection, and it cannot be $SHELL.  Run programs by short name from a  directory on PATH, or `load' them first and fork them by name.ksh -- the one programs need----------------------------  The only shell here that takes a whole command line as one argument,  which is what makes it the right $SHELL.  `ksh -c '<commands>'` works  too, and its `cd' moves the data directory.  Typed at, it reads a line with read(fd, buf, 256).  An os9exec whose  I$Read waited for the full count, rather than returning at end of  record, never saw a typed command; a current one gives a full shell --  prompt, for loops, variables, forking.  DOC/README-KSH has the rest.  Its `read' was rebuilt on 2026-09-27: a second `while read ...; done  < file' in a script reads its file, and a run of spaces between words  is one separator, as in sh.  SRC/pdksh/README.OSK has the details.  It needs nothing besides its own file.  At start-up os9exec prints  four `F$SetSys: unimplemented 03D8' lines; they are the emulator's  noise, not an error.gshell and mshell -- menus--------------------------  `gshell' draws a full-screen file browser: a lettered list of the  current directory, `+' and `-' to page, `.' to change directory, a  letter to run a file and its lower-case twin to run it with parameters.  `assembler', `compiler' and `editor' on this disk are the same engine  pointed at one job each.  Help text in DOC/gshell.  `mshell' reads a menu file named on its command line, and says  `Could not open <name> (menufile)' without one.  Both want TERM set.  Take one to hand somebody a machine that runs four  things and nothing else, not to type at.HOW A SINGLE-ARGUMENT COMMAND LINE FARES----------------------------------------Handed `/dd/CMDS/wc -c /dd/SYS/motd' as one argument (measured2026-09-27):  ksh      40 /dd/SYS/motd -- it parses it  bash     "cannot execute binary file" -- it reads a script filename  sh       "/dd/CMDS/wc: file not found"  gshell   opens its menu with `Directory : /dd/CMDS/wc -c /dd/SYS/motd'  mshell   "Too many items in /dd/CMDS/wc -c /dd/SYS/motd (> 10)"A program that examines $SHELL itself needs the first.  SYS/login setsSHELL to ksh for that reason, and bash reads it too.SYSTEM() FORKS `shell', NOT $SHELL----------------------------------Microware's C library system() forks the module named `shell', acompiled-in constant.  SHELL set to ksh, to sh, to Microware's shell, orunset, gives the same result.  None of the five here is named `shell', sowith nothing else resident the fork fails with E_MNF and nothing runs.The return value tells you nothing: it has been a junk address that movesbetween runs, and 0, which reads as success.The sound test is whether a redirect target was created.  A shell makesthe file after `>' before it runs the command, so `prog > out' leaves`out' behind even when `prog' does not exist.  No file means no shellread the string.On a real OS-9 system /dd/CMDS/shell is always there and this neverarises.  Under an emulator, SYS/login makes your own resident, along withrunb and the other commands of yours that programs here run by name:    load /h1/CMDS/<name> >/nil 2>/nilThat takes each from your system, mounted as /h1.  Nothing of Microware'sis shipped for it.  With no /h1 each load finds nothing, and programsthat shell out do nothing.Microware's shell wants its own redirection syntax:    cat file > out     `^syntax error', and system() returns 0 anyway    cat file >out      worksA space before `>' fails silently.  ksh takes both spellings.What is affected, measured program by program:  m4         syscmd is silent without a `shell' module and works with one.             The only shipped program found affected.  ed         its `!' fails earlier, for its own reason: it opens its             scratch file as /r0/ed.XXXXXX (SRC/ed/buf.c).  RCS ci     builds `>-tmpfile', Microware redirection no shell here             parses.  gawk       refuses system(): "the system function is not supported".  make       unaffected; it calls os9exec() directly and prints each             command line as it runs it.  tex        unaffected, and so are latex, eo and maketexpk: identical             output with SHELL set or unset, and with or without a             `shell' module.The web server WN runs a CGI program through a shell.  With SHELL unsetevery CGI request answers    HTTP/1.0 500 Can't exec CGI programand nothing else says why: the CGI module runs when you type its name,its attributes are right, and so is its index.cache entry.  With SHELLset the same request returns 200 and the program's output (measured2026-09-23 on WN's own examples under c/unid/wn_1.14.3/osk/EXAMPLES).A SCRIPT AT THE END OF A PIPE WAITS FOR EVER--------------------------------------------      cat notes | bash myfilter         never finishes      cat notes | myprogram             finishesA shell whose standard input is a pipe keeps that path open while itwaits for the command it started.  OS-9's pipe manager gives end-of-fileonly when the pipe is empty and every process holding the path isreading it; the waiting shell holds it without reading, so the commandinside the script never sees end-of-file.  Every shell here does this,Microware's included.  It is the pipe manager's documented rule, seen onos9exec and not yet checked on hardware.  Give the script the file as anargument, or put the program itself in the pipe.